This function compiles without errors. What happens when plain JavaScript calls `area("5")` on the emitted code?

```ts
export function area(side: number): number {
  return side * side;
}
```

Under `"strict": true`, why does this report `'e' is of type 'unknown'`?

```ts
try {
  JSON.parse(input);
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e.message);
}
```

With this tsconfig, `src/index.ts` contains `import { v } from "@lib/util";` and type-checks. What import does the emitted `dist/index.js` contain?

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "outDir": "dist", "rootDir": "src",
    "paths": { "@lib/*": ["./src/lib/*"] }
  }
}
```

A Node package has `"type": "module"` and this tsconfig. Why does the import fail to compile, and what is the fix?

```json
{ "compilerOptions": { "module": "nodenext" } }
```
```ts
// src/a.ts
import { b } from "./b";
```

Under `strict`, which assignment is an error?

```ts
type Handlers = {
  onA(x: string | number): void;           // method syntax
  onB: (x: string | number) => void;       // property syntax
};
const h: Handlers = {
  onA(x: string) {},
  onB: (x: string) => {},
};
```

In this snippet, `@ts-ignore` hides only the first error on the next line, so tsc still reports the second call.

```ts
function add(a: number, b: number) { return a + b; }
// @ts-ignore
add(1, "3"); add(1, "4");
```

With `"target": "es2017"` and `"module": "commonjs"`, which statement about the emitted code for this class is correct?

```ts
export const pick = (v?: string) => v ?? "default";
export class Counter { #count = 0; inc() { return ++this.#count; } }
```

With `"module": "commonjs"`, this emits `const fs_1 = __importDefault(require("fs"))` and then reads `fs_1.default`. `fs` is a CommonJS module with no `__esModule` flag. What does `fs_1.default` hold?

```ts
import fs from "fs";
fs.readFileSync("a.txt");
```

A file is compiled with `"module": "commonjs"` and `"verbatimModuleSyntax": true`. tsc reports TS1295: "ECMAScript imports and exports cannot be written in a CommonJS file". Why, and what is a valid fix?

```ts
import fs from "fs";
export const x = 1;
```

A project enables `"isolatedModules": true`. Which use of `Dir.Up` is reported as an error?

```ts
// types.d.ts
declare const enum Dir { Up = 1 }

// local.ts
export const enum Mode { On = 1 }
export const m = Mode.On;

// app.ts
export const d = Dir.Up;
```

This config builds `dist/a.js`. A developer deletes `dist/` and runs `tsc -p .` again. tsc exits 0 but writes nothing. Why?

```json
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "incremental": true, "outDir": "dist", "rootDir": ".",
    "tsBuildInfoFile": ".cache/app.tsbuildinfo"
  }
}
```

This file is in a project with `"allowJs": true, "checkJs": true`. Which two statements are true? (Choose two.)

```js
// math.js
/** @param {number} a @param {number} b */
export function add(a, b) { return a + b; }
add(1, "2");
/** @type {string} */
export let label = 5;
```

A bundled app uses `"module": "preserve"`. Which two statements about this file are correct? (Choose two.)

```ts
import fs = require("fs");
import { join } from "path";
export const exists = fs.existsSync(join("data", "a.json"));
```

tsc reports TS2610 on `x = 1` ("x is defined as an accessor in class Base, but is overridden here as an instance property"). If the code is emitted anyway with `"target": "es2022"`, what does `new D()` do at runtime?

```ts
class Base {
  get x() { return 0; }
  set x(v: number) { console.log("setter", v); }
}
class D extends Base { x = 1; }
```

Under default TypeScript 6 settings, which function produces an error, and why?

```ts
function a(x: number): number { if (x > 0) return 1; }
function b(x: number) { if (x > 0) return 1; }
```

A team sets `"strict": true, "exactOptionalPropertyTypes": true`. Which line is an error?

```ts
type User = { id: number; nickname?: string };
function update(u: User, patch: Partial<User>): User {
  return { ...u, ...patch };
}
const p: Partial<User> = { id: undefined };
```
